    free: false,
    bodyHtml:
      "<p>Chapter 4 covered piloting business development manually. This is what it looks like once you turn that pilot into a repeatable weekly system, using Claude as the engine instead of a dedicated BD platform.</p><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"circle\">1</div><div class=\"body\"><b>Signal detection.</b><span>A standing weekly search for the specific signals that predict a client for you — funding rounds, exec hires, new job postings in your niche — the same search from the Chapter 3 quick start, run on a schedule instead of once.</span></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"circle\">2</div><div class=\"body\"><b>Outreach generation.</b><span>Draft messages grounded in the specific signal you found, not a template with a name swapped in. The reply-rate gap from Chapter 1 comes from relevance, not clever copy.</span></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"circle\">3</div><div class=\"body\"><b>Cadence tracking.</b><span>A simple log — who you contacted, on what signal, and whether they replied — is enough to know if the system is working. It doesn't need to be a CRM until volume outgrows a spreadsheet.</span></div></div></div><h3>A Weekly Rhythm That Actually Sticks</h3><table><tr><th>Day</th><th>Task</th></tr><tr><td>Monday</td><td>Run the signal search for the week — 15-20 new companies</td></tr><tr><td>Tuesday-Wednesday</td><td>Draft and send outreach for that batch, grounded in each specific signal</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Log replies from last week's batch, update the cadence tracker</td></tr><tr><td>Friday</td><td>Review reply rate against the Chapter 1 benchmark; adjust next week's signal criteria if it's off</td></tr></table><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"head\">The part most agencies skip</div><p>BD is the first thing that gets dropped when delivery gets busy. Protecting the Monday signal search specifically — even 30 minutes — keeps the pipeline from going quiet three weeks later when this month's reqs are filled.</p></div>",
