      "<p>Whatever you piloted in Part 3, don't stop measuring once it's live. Each juncture has one or two numbers that tell you whether it's still earning its spot in the stack from Chapter 2.</p><table><tr><th>Juncture</th><th>KPI to track</th></tr><tr><td>Business development</td><td>Reply rate, meetings booked, job orders won per week</td></tr><tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>Time to 10-candidate shortlist per req</td></tr><tr><td>Screening</td><td>Agreement rate between AI ranking and final hire</td></tr><tr><td>Scheduling / intelligence</td><td>Hours saved on notes per week</td></tr><tr><td>Reference checks</td><td>Turnaround time in hours</td></tr><tr><td>Rediscovery</td><td>% of placements sourced from existing database</td></tr><tr><td>Account management</td><td>Repeat-client rate, revenue per active client</td></tr></table><h3>How Often to Review</h3><ul class=\"icon-list\"><li>Weekly, for the first month after any new tool goes live — you're still catching setup issues, not just measuring performance</li><li>Monthly after that, as part of a standing team check-in, not a special event</li><li>Immediately after any client or team feedback that a juncture \"feels\" slower — check the number before assuming the feeling is right or wrong</li></ul><p>Review these monthly, not just during the initial pilot. A tool that clears the bar in week one can quietly stop earning its cost six months later if your req mix or team size changes — the checklist in Chapter 13 applies to renewals, not just new purchases.</p>",
