    subtitle: "What Goes Wrong and Why",
    free: false,
    bodyHtml:
      "<p>These are the four objections that come up most often before an agency starts, and the two failure modes that show up most often after. None of them are reasons to skip AI adoption — they're reasons to do it in the order this guide lays out.</p><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">\"Candidates will hate an AI-screened process\"</div><p>Candidates prefer a fast, clear response over a slow human one. Most candidate frustration with hiring isn't about who or what reviewed their application — it's silence. A fast rejection with a real reason beats a three-week wait for any answer at all.</p></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">\"This is too expensive for a small agency\"</div><p>A lean stack runs under $300/month. See Chapter 2 for the exact breakdown — it's less than the commission on a fraction of one placement, and every juncture in Part 3 has a free or near-free starting tier.</p></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">\"Clients won't trust AI-screened candidates\"</div><p>They will if you can explain the scorecard behind it. That's what the scorecard habit from Chapter 6 and Chapter 14 is for — a client asking \"why this candidate\" gets a real answer, not \"the algorithm said so.\"</p></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">\"We tried something like this before and it didn't work\"</div><p>Ask what \"this\" actually was. Most failed AI rollouts turn out to be the failure mode below — several junctures added at once, none piloted properly — not evidence that a specific, well-piloted juncture won't work.</p></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">The real failure mode: adding everything at once</div><p>50-70% of teams that deploy AI outbound tools churn within 3 months. Chapter 12's rollout sequence exists specifically to avoid this. One juncture, properly piloted and measured, beats eight junctures added in the same month and abandoned in the same quarter.</p></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"head\">The quieter failure mode: piloting, then never reviewing again</div><p>A tool that passed its pilot in month one can stop earning its cost by month six if nobody's watching the Chapter 16 KPIs. Treat every tool as provisional, not permanent, from day one.</p></div>",
